We are going to look for rainfall data collected in Lenton (just by university park), this is our sample that we hope/assume is representative of the population (whole of UK) rainfall.
We need to download this data from https://nrfa.ceh.ac.uk/data/station/meanflow/28035 (we can't download this data directly from Moodle due to the data license).  Toward the bottom left click `Download catchment daily rainfall data`, you then need to complete a form with four questions (your answers are likely to be university student, Teaching/Education, other and other) and accept the terms and conditions (which are worth having a quick read through to see how real data is often used and licensed). Once you have completed this the data should download.

If you look at this data by opening it in excel you can see it isn't in the neat form you are used to working in. The top of the data isn't actual data but information on the dataset that we won't want to load into R. And so we can run
```{r set up, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE}
library(readr)
rainfall <- read_csv("28035_cdr.csv", col_names = c('date', 'rf'), skip = 20)
```
this skips the first 20 rows, and also adds columns names 'date' and 'rf' (standing for rainfall).

It may be interesting to group our data by year or month and so the below code creates new columns that assign a variable for the year and the month the data was collected
``` {r date}
rainfall$year <- format(as.Date(rainfall$date, format="%d/%m/%Y"),"%Y")
rainfall$month <- format(as.Date(rainfall$date, format="%d/%m/%Y"),"%m")
```

Some example of then creating datasets for particular years (2012 and 1990) or particular months (Febuary and July)
``` {r date groups}
rainfall_2012 <- rainfall[rainfall$year=='2012',]$rf
rainfall_1990 <- rainfall[rainfall$year=='1990',]$rf

rainfall_Feb <- rainfall[rainfall$month=='02',]$rf
rainfall_Jul <- rainfall[rainfall$month=='07',]$rf
```

Have a go at visually exploring this dataset, you could plot rainfall for a certain year, compare two years or months or something else!

Some ideas of the types of plots you may produce
```{r exploring data}
plot(rainfall_2012, type='l')

hist(rainfall_2012,col =rgb(1,0,0,0.5), breaks=seq(0,50,1))
hist(rainfall_1990, add=TRUE, col =rgb(0,0,1,0.5), breaks=seq(0,50,1))


boxplot(rainfall_1990, at=0.5, xlim=c(0,2), names=c('1990'), main='boxplot for rainfall in 1990 (left) and 2012 (right)')
boxplot(rainfall_2012, add=TRUE, at=1.5, names='2012')
```

The daily rainfall plot for 2012 is hard to see any trends as it is so noisy, and it is being dominated by the more extreme values.
The histogram has 2012 in red and 1990 in blue, but doesn't show much due to a high overlap. The boxplot's look very similar although again the extreme values are dominating it so perhaps it would be better to produce a more zoomed in version of this.

